  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y-{x}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\u27e8\\,,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2z\u27e9=\\lambda \u27e8\\,,\\,1,\\,0\u27e9+\\mu \u27e82x,\\,,\\,2z\u27e9 \\\\ y-{x}^{2}=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?",
